WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:10.928
<v Jamie>Tesla's down twelve point four two percent, the Nasdaq's off one point seven three percent, and somehow the actual story is a one billion dollar fine that dropped yesterday—but today it's personal.

00:00:10.928 --> 00:00:25.830
<v Maya>So yesterday there's talk of geopolitical risk from China, and today the EU just slapped Google with a one billion dollar fine for crushing competition—which, by the way, is exactly what the U.S. is about to accuse China of doing. The math just changed.
  ↳ source: https://www.theverge.com/tech/943866/google-alphabet-eu-dma-fine-search-services-play-store-steering

00:00:25.830 --> 00:00:47.686
<v Alex>The S&P dropped zero point nine four percent, Nasdaq fell one point seven three percent, and Tesla got absolutely demolished—down twelve point four two percent. But here's the real story: the V I X spiked twelve point four four percent, which means traders are pricing in volatility again. The ten-year's climbing, and that's the inflation monster waking up from a nap it shouldn't have taken.

00:00:47.686 --> 00:00:53.316
<v Jamie>So we went from 'maybe the Fed cuts rates' to 'everything hurts' in like thirty-six hours.

00:00:53.316 --> 00:01:07.224
<v Alex>Exactly. Tesla's down on geopolitical concerns. Meanwhile Thermo Fisher's up nine point seven two percent, energy stocks are rallying, and defensive plays are printing money. The market's saying: inflation's back on the menu, growth's questionable, so give me dividends and healthcare.

00:01:07.224 --> 00:01:12.192
<v Maya>That's not a geopolitical reset—that's a geopolitical speed bump that just became a pothole.

00:01:12.192 --> 00:01:19.808
<v Jamie>Google's down five point ninety-seven percent on top of that EU fine, so tech's getting hit from both sides—regulation and macro headwinds.

00:01:19.808 --> 00:01:35.373
<v Alex>Google, Amazon, Meta—all down three to six percent. Rates are going up, inflation expectations are rising, and the one thing that was supposed to carry the rally just got a lot more expensive to own. That's the market telling you it's repricing everything from the top down.

00:01:35.373 --> 00:01:50.275
<v Maya>The fine is about Google blocking app developers from showing customers cheaper payment options outside the store for years—that's not a slip-up, that's a business model. The EU said that's anticompetitive, so now Google has to let developers link out. That's a revenue leak.

00:01:50.275 --> 00:01:53.255
<v Alex>How big of a leak are we talking?

00:01:53.255 --> 00:02:14.780
<v Maya>Google takes a thirty percent cut on Play Store transactions. If even five percent of developers start routing people to cheaper checkout outside the store, that's hundreds of millions a year. But the real damage is precedent—the EU just proved it can make Google restructure how it makes money, and it did it without waiting for the U.S. to figure out what it thinks.
  ↳ source: https://www.theverge.com/tech/943866/google-alphabet-eu-dma-fine-search-services-play-store-steering

00:02:14.780 --> 00:02:21.734
<v Jamie>So the EU swung first, and now there's talk of tariffs. These are two different weapons aimed at the same companies.

00:02:21.734 --> 00:02:43.260
<v Alex>The timing is brutal because tech stocks are already repricing on rates and inflation. This EU fine says the regulatory environment just got harder to predict—you can't lobby your way out of it, you can't wait for a friendly administration. Google's got to change the product. Amazon's watching this. Meta's watching this. Everyone's asking: what else is the EU going to redefine as anticompetitive?

00:02:43.260 --> 00:02:54.188
<v Jamie>And then tariffs could hit, which means tech hardware margins get crushed, supply chains get weird, and suddenly you're managing both a regulatory rewrite and a cost shock at the same time.

00:02:54.188 --> 00:03:09.421
<v Maya>That's the moment these companies realize they can't just hedge geopolitical risk with optimism and lobbying anymore. The EU proved it moves independent of Washington. You're not playing one game with one referee—you're playing three games with three referees who aren't talking to each other.

00:03:09.421 --> 00:03:18.693
<v Alex>Blackstone just posted record inflows on the back of a Q2 earnings beat—AI investments are printing money, and they're raising capital faster than they can deploy it.

00:03:18.693 --> 00:03:27.635
<v Jamie>So they're crushing it on the AI narrative right now, but what happens when the market reprices and people actually want returns instead of just exposure?

00:03:27.635 --> 00:03:41.212
<v Alex>That's the billion-dollar question. Blackstone's got the brand and the dry powder, but they're betting they can deploy capital into AI infrastructure before the hype cycle corrects. If they're wrong about timing, those record inflows turn into redemption pressure real fast.

00:03:41.212 --> 00:03:53.465
<v Maya>I B M just told everyone that AI isn't actually killing the mainframe—which is like a restaurant insisting the food poisoning rumors are overblown while the health inspector is literally still in the kitchen writing citations.

00:03:53.465 --> 00:03:57.770
<v Jamie>Their stock crashed on mainframe warnings, and now they're walking it back?

00:03:57.770 --> 00:04:09.360
<v Maya>They're saying AI temporarily wrecked corporate hardware budgets, but the mainframe business will recover. The problem is nobody believes them—the quarter already happened. You don't get to reframe the earnings miss as a feature.

00:04:09.360 --> 00:04:16.977
<v Alex>Albertsons stock just tanked because grocery spending is softening. They lowered their full-year outlook, which means the consumer is finally tapping out.
  ↳ source: https://www.cnbc.com/2026/07/23/albertsons-stock-plunges-on-lowered-outlook-softened-grocery-trends.html

00:04:16.977 --> 00:04:23.931
<v Jamie>That's the canary in the coal mine, right? If people are cutting back on groceries, everything else is coming down.

00:04:23.931 --> 00:04:34.197
<v Alex>Exactly. Grocers are the first to feel demand destruction because food's non-discretionary—you can't not eat. When Albertsons sees weakness, it means the middle of the income distribution is already pulling back.

00:04:34.197 --> 00:04:42.807
<v Maya>And meanwhile the ten-year Treasury yield just hit its highest level since January because oil's screaming higher and traders are suddenly worried about inflation again.
  ↳ source: https://www.cnbc.com/2026/07/23/treasury-yields-oil-prices-jobless-claims.html

00:04:42.807 --> 00:04:53.735
<v Alex>Oil's up, and the Fed's entire rate-cut thesis just got a lot quieter. The narrative shifted from 'we're cutting because growth is soft' to 'actually, maybe we're not cutting because prices are rising.'

00:04:53.735 --> 00:05:04.332
<v Jamie>So you've got consumers pulling back on groceries, Blackstone dumping money into AI infrastructure, and the bond market saying inflation's the real problem. That's three different markets pricing three different futures.

00:05:04.332 --> 00:05:12.942
<v Alex>If Albertsons is pulling back, the airline industry's going to have a rough quarter. Leisure travel holds up longer than groceries, but not by much.

00:05:12.942 --> 00:05:22.215
<v Maya>Albertsons reports on July twenty-third, and they've already warned the market once. A second miss in a row means the consumer isn't just tightening—they're in triage mode.

00:05:22.215 --> 00:05:31.156
<v Jamie>So we get two different views of how broke people actually are: one from the grocery aisle, one from the airport gate, both landing at once.

00:05:31.156 --> 00:05:38.773
<v Alex>Google just learned that being the biggest player in the room doesn't protect you when the room decides to rewrite the rules.

00:05:38.773 --> 00:05:45.065
<v Maya>And when tariffs land, every tech company's going to wish they'd paid attention to the EU's memo first.

00:05:45.065 --> 00:05:49.701
<v Jamie>This show is for entertainment and education only — nothing here is investment advice.
